WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.876
Task statement 3.1.

2
00:00:01.876 --> 00:00:08.251
Configure CLAUDE.md files with appropriate hierarchy, scoping, and modular organization.

3
00:00:08.251 --> 00:00:16.436
A new teammate's Claude ignores your test conventions because the rule lives in your personal file, not the repo.

4
00:00:17.836 --> 00:00:24.222
CLAUDE dot md is simply a markdown file of instructions that Claude reads every time a session starts.

5
00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:32.235
Think of three layers: a team handbook in the repository, your own notebook at home, and sticky notes inside particular folders.

6
00:00:32.235 --> 00:00:37.932
Claude reads all of them together, so the question is always which layer a rule belongs in.

7
00:00:39.332 --> 00:00:45.718
The user file lives in your home folder and follows you to every project, but nobody else ever sees it.

8
00:00:45.718 --> 00:00:51.483
The project file sits at the repository root and travels with git, so the whole team gets it.

9
00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:59.791
Files in subfolders load when Claude starts reading or editing files in that folder, which keeps unrelated conventions out of the way.

10
00:01:01.191 --> 00:01:03.468
As instructions grow, split them up.

11
00:01:03.468 --> 00:01:10.678
An at-sign import pulls another file into CLAUDE dot md, and the dot claude rules folder holds one topic per file.

12
00:01:10.678 --> 00:01:15.169
The memory slash command lists your memory files so you can check them.

13
00:01:15.169 --> 00:01:22.823
Current docs also point to the context command for confirming exactly what loaded, but the exam keeps the memory wording.

14
00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:30.424
In the code generation scenario, a new developer joins and their Claude keeps writing tests the old way.

15
00:01:30.424 --> 00:01:32.392
Nothing is broken in their setup.

16
00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:37.163
The rule was written into your user level file, which never leaves your machine.

17
00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:44.319
Treat it as a hierarchy problem: move the rule to the committed project file, then confirm it shows up in their session.

18
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:51.517
Watch for answers that fix the symptom for one person instead of fixing where the rule lives.

19
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:55.133
Watch too for one huge file that tries to hold everything.

20
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:58.562
And remember that CLAUDE dot md is advice to the model.

21
00:01:58.562 --> 00:02:03.425
If something must never happen, that is a job for a hook or a permission rule.

22
00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:08.334
Three layers, all combined: user, project and directory.

23
00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:17.230
Personal preferences go in your home folder, team standards go in the repository, and folder specific notes go next to the code they describe.

24
00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:24.623
When one person's Claude is missing a rule, check which layer the rule was written into before changing anything else.

